// bbqueue — a single-producer single-consumer byte queue whose unit of
// work is a CONTIGUOUS GRANT rather than a byte.
//
// WHAT A GRANT IS.  The producer asks for N contiguous bytes, is told
// where they are, writes them where they are, and then commits M of the
// N.  The consumer is told where the readable bytes are, reads them
// where they are, and releases K of them.  Nothing is copied into the
// queue and nothing is copied out: the write IS the frame, and the
// commit is what makes it visible.  That is the whole reason to prefer
// this to push and pop, and the README argues it.
//
// THE WRAP IS A WATERMARK, NEVER A SPLIT GRANT.  A grant is contiguous
// or it does not exist.  When the tail of the buffer is too small for
// what the producer asked for, the grant is taken from the FRONT
// instead and the watermark records where the written region ended — so
// the consumer knows to stop there and wrap, and no caller ever has to
// handle a frame in two pieces.  A frame in two pieces is exactly what
// a ring buffer forces on an interrupt handler and exactly what this
// does not.
//
// SANS-IO, AND THEREFORE BUFFERLESS.  The queue is index arithmetic and
// nothing else: it owns no bytes, reads none and writes none.  The
// buffer belongs to the caller, and a grant is a pair of indices into
// it.  That is what lets one queue serve a `Bytes` on a host and a
// static region on a device without knowing which it is — and it is
// also the honest shape rather than the chosen one, because a `@value`
// struct is built whole and replaced whole (SPEC § 14.3), so a queue
// that owned its buffer would copy the whole buffer on every byte
// written.  The README says what the language would need for the other
// shape.
//
// NO LOCK, NO ATOMIC, NO EFFECT.  Every function here is a total
// function from values to values, so there is nothing in this module to
// protect.  What needs protecting is the CELL two contexts share the
// queue value through, and that belongs to the caller; the README says
// how the runtime's critical section meets it, and why the section is
// around one field and not around the queue.

// The queue between two operations: four indices and a flag, and no
// bytes at all.
//
// A `@value` struct, so it lives in the caller's stack frame or its
// static cell with no header, no reference count and no allocation.
//
// `write_at` is where the producer's next grant begins and `read_at` is
// where the consumer's next one does.  `inverted` is true when the
// producer has wrapped past the consumer — when the written region is
// `read_at..watermark` followed by `0..write_at` rather than the single
// run `read_at..write_at`.  `watermark` is where the written region
// ended before that wrap, and is `cap` while the queue is not inverted.
//
// The flag is a field rather than a comparison of the two indices
// because `write_at == read_at` is both "empty" and "full" and the
// comparison cannot tell them apart.  One bit spent on saying which is
// cheaper than any of the ways of avoiding it.
pub @value
struct BbQueue
    cap: Int
    write_at: Int
    read_at: Int
    watermark: Int
    inverted: Bool

// A run of contiguous bytes in the caller's buffer: where it starts,
// and how many bytes are in it.
//
// `len` of 0 is "there is no room" for a write grant and "there is
// nothing to read" for a read grant.  It is not a failure and there is
// no error value: a producer that is ahead of its consumer is the
// normal state of a log queue, and a caller that has to `match` on it
// would write the same `if` either way.
//
// A grant is handed back to `commit` or `release` rather than only its
// length, because the queue has to know WHERE the grant was: a grant
// taken from the front of the buffer is the wrap, and the wrap is what
// moves the watermark.
pub @value
struct BbGrant
    at: Int
    len: Int

// ── The room a grant comes from ──────────────────────────────────────

// How many contiguous bytes are free where the producer's next grant
// begins.
//
// Before the wrap that is the rest of the buffer.  After it the
// producer is writing at the front of the buffer, so its run ends where
// the reader has got to rather than at the capacity.
fn tail_room(q: BbQueue) -> Int
    if q.inverted
        q.read_at - q.write_at
    else
        q.cap - q.write_at

// How many contiguous bytes are free at the front of the buffer, which
// is where a grant goes when the tail is too short for it.
//
// Zero once the queue is inverted, because the front is then written
// bytes and not free space.  That is what lets a queue be full while
// bytes are free: those bytes are behind the reader, and the only run a
// grant can come from is the one the producer is standing in.  Before
// the wrap the front is exactly what the reader has released.
fn front_room(q: BbQueue) -> Int
    if q.inverted
        0
    else
        q.read_at

// A fresh queue over a caller's buffer of `cap` bytes.
//
// The buffer is not passed and not touched.  `cap` is the caller's
// promise about the buffer it will pass to every read and write it
// makes with this queue, and every index this module answers is inside
// `0..cap`.
//
// ```novo
// use bbqueue
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let q = bbqueue.queue(256)
//     println("${bbqueue.capacity(q)} ${bbqueue.len(q)}")   // 256 0
// ```
pub fn queue(cap: Int) -> BbQueue
    BbQueue { cap: cap, write_at: 0, read_at: 0, watermark: cap, inverted: false }

// Ask for `want` contiguous bytes, and answer where they are.
//
// `len` is `want` when the request fits and 0 when it does not; a
// partial grant is never given, because a caller that asked for a whole
// frame's worth of room cannot use half of it.  `at` is meaningful only
// when `len` is above 0.
//
// THE WRAP HAPPENS HERE.  When the tail of the buffer is too short but
// the front is long enough, the grant is taken from the front — `at` is
// 0 — and `commit` is what records the watermark.  The queue is
// unchanged by this call: asking does not reserve, and a producer that
// asks twice without committing gets the same answer twice.
//
// ```novo
// use bbqueue
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let q = bbqueue.queue(64)
//     let g = bbqueue.grant(q, 8)
//     println("${g.at} ${g.len}")   // 0 8
// ```
pub fn grant(q: BbQueue, want: Int) -> BbGrant
    if want <= tail_room(q)
        BbGrant { at: q.write_at, len: want }
    elif want <= front_room(q)
        BbGrant { at: 0, len: want }
    else
        BbGrant { at: 0, len: 0 }

// The largest contiguous grant available, which may be 0.
//
// What a producer with a variable-length frame asks for: give me
// whatever is there, and I will write what fits.  It is the same
// arithmetic as `grant`, asked the other way round.
//
// ```novo
// use bbqueue
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let q = bbqueue.queue(64)
//     println("${bbqueue.grant_max(q).len}")   // 64
// ```
pub fn grant_max(q: BbQueue) -> BbGrant
    let tail = tail_room(q)
    let front = front_room(q)
    if tail >= front
        BbGrant { at: q.write_at, len: tail }
    else
        BbGrant { at: 0, len: front }

// Make the first `used` bytes of `g` visible to the consumer, and
// answer the queue after it.
//
// `used` may be less than `g.len` — that is the point of the two-step:
// a producer reserves what a frame might need and commits what it
// turned out to be, and the difference is given back rather than
// wasted.  `used` above `g.len` is a panic, exactly as `xs[i]` past the
// end is: a caller that committed more than it asked for is a mistake
// in the program, not a value the program should carry around.
//
// This is the ONLY function that moves `write_at`, `watermark` or
// `inverted`, and `release` is the only one that moves `read_at`.  That
// division is what makes the queue safe across an interrupt boundary
// with one word of protection rather than a lock — see the README.
//
// ```novo
// use bbqueue
//
// fn main() [io]
//     var q = bbqueue.queue(64)
//     let g = bbqueue.grant(q, 8)
//     // ... the caller writes g.len bytes at g.at ...
//     q = bbqueue.commit(q, g, 5)
//     println("${bbqueue.len(q)}")   // 5
// ```
pub fn commit(q: BbQueue, g: BbGrant, used: Int) -> BbQueue
    if used > g.len
        panic.panic("bbqueue.commit: used is above the grant")
    // A grant at 0 is the wrap only when the buffer already held bytes.
    // On an empty queue the run at 0 is the tail run, and reading the
    // two the same way would set a watermark of 0 and hide the commit
    // from `read`.
    if not q.inverted and g.at == 0 and q.write_at > 0
        BbQueue { cap: q.cap, write_at: used, read_at: q.read_at,
            watermark: q.write_at, inverted: true }
    else
        BbQueue { cap: q.cap, write_at: g.at + used, read_at: q.read_at,
            watermark: q.watermark, inverted: q.inverted }

// The contiguous bytes waiting to be read, which may be none.
//
// `len` stops at the watermark when the queue is inverted, so a
// consumer never sees a run that spans the wrap.  Two calls with a
// `release` between them drain a wrapped queue in two grants, and that
// is the only place the wrap is visible to a caller at all.
//
// ```novo
// use bbqueue
//
// fn main() [io]
//     var q = bbqueue.queue(64)
//     let w = bbqueue.grant(q, 8)
//     q = bbqueue.commit(q, w, 8)
//     let r = bbqueue.read(q)
//     println("${r.at} ${r.len}")   // 0 8
// ```
pub fn read(q: BbQueue) -> BbGrant
    if q.inverted
        BbGrant { at: q.read_at, len: q.watermark - q.read_at }
    else
        BbGrant { at: q.read_at, len: q.write_at - q.read_at }

// Give the first `used` bytes of `g` back to the producer, and answer
// the queue after it.
//
// `used` may be less than `g.len`, so a consumer that drained one whole
// frame out of a grant holding three releases one frame's worth and
// comes back for the rest.  `used` above `g.len` is a panic, for the
// reason `commit` gives.
//
// Releasing up to the watermark of an inverted queue is what clears the
// inversion: `read_at` returns to 0, `watermark` returns to `cap`, and
// the next `read` sees the run the producer wrote at the front.
//
// ```novo
// use bbqueue
//
// fn main() [io]
//     var q = bbqueue.queue(64)
//     let w = bbqueue.grant(q, 8)
//     q = bbqueue.commit(q, w, 8)
//     let r = bbqueue.read(q)
//     q = bbqueue.release(q, r, 8)
//     println("${bbqueue.len(q)}")   // 0
// ```
pub fn release(q: BbQueue, g: BbGrant, used: Int) -> BbQueue
    if used > g.len
        panic.panic("bbqueue.release: used is above the grant")
    let next_read = g.at + used
    if q.inverted and next_read == q.watermark
        BbQueue { cap: q.cap, write_at: q.write_at, read_at: 0,
            watermark: q.cap, inverted: false }
    else
        BbQueue { cap: q.cap, write_at: q.write_at, read_at: next_read,
            watermark: q.watermark, inverted: q.inverted }

// How many bytes are committed and not yet released, across the wrap.
//
// This is the queue's depth and not the length of the next read grant —
// an inverted queue answers the sum of its two runs here and the length
// of the first of them from `read`.  A caller watching for backpressure
// wants this one; a caller about to read wants `read`.
pub fn len(q: BbQueue) -> Int
    if q.inverted
        q.watermark - q.read_at + q.write_at
    else
        q.write_at - q.read_at

// The buffer size the queue was made for.
pub fn capacity(q: BbQueue) -> Int
    q.cap

// True when nothing is committed and unreleased.
pub fn is_empty(q: BbQueue) -> Bool
    len(q) == 0

// True when no grant of any size can be given.
//
// A queue can be full with bytes free: the free space is on the wrong
// side of the wrap, or it is split, and a contiguous grant needs a
// contiguous run.  That is the cost of the guarantee, and it is why
// this is a function rather than `len(q) == capacity(q)`.
pub fn is_full(q: BbQueue) -> Bool
    grant_max(q).len == 0

// Where the written region ends when the queue is inverted, and the
// capacity when it is not.
//
// Published because a caller debugging a wrap needs to see it, and
// because the field is the one part of this design a reader is most
// likely to want to check against the reference implementation.
pub fn watermark(q: BbQueue) -> Int
    q.watermark

// True when the producer has wrapped past the consumer.
//
// The state the watermark exists for.  A caller does not need to
// consult it — `read` and `grant` already account for it — and it is
// published for the same reason `watermark` is.
pub fn is_inverted(q: BbQueue) -> Bool
    q.inverted
